title: The Cincinnati Banner Holds
subtitle: The <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republicans**</span> somehow march on
tags: event
view-if: liberal_reunion_triggered = 0 and liberal_split = 1 and year = 1873 and month = 2
on-arrival: liberal_survive = 1

= Willard Hotel Rally

After the 1872 election, the leading members of the <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republican Party**</span> decide to keep their organization alive. They see an opportunity to remain a national force against <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> corruption, patronage and expanding federal power.

It remains to be seen how long this experiment can last. Delegates gather at the Willard Hotel, not to mourn Greeley's defeat but to argue over what comes next. Carl Schurz insists that civil service reform, fiscal restraint, hard money and an end to military Reconstruction are more urgent now than they were in 1872. Abandoning the organization, he argues, would hand the field back to the same corruption they set out to fight.

Others in the room are already looking past the <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> entirely. Quiet conversations turn toward the <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic Party**</span>, and whether men like Samuel Tilden, who share the <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberals'**</span> contempt for <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span>-era corruption without sharing the <span style="color: #002765;">**Bourbon**</span> South's appetite for reversing Reconstruction outright, might eventually make better partners than either of the parties that already exist. No one says so out loud. Not yet.

By the end of the meeting, the national committee votes narrowly to keep the party's organization intact, its officers in place and its press allies writing. It is a decision to survive, not a plan for how. The next real test will come with the 1874 midterms, when the country will learn whether the <span style="color: #DAA520;">**Liberal Republican**</span> experiment has a future or is merely a footnote in history.

- @damn: Shame...

@damn

The gavel falls, and the delegates file out into the Washington cold, still a party, for now.